  "surprise": "P20 reversed / modded-nanogpt port (no LR warmup: the WARMUP knob and linear ramp deleted, 186-claude otherwise unchanged, -2 lines) scored 694/694/663 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 186's 668/699/674: mean 683.67 vs 680.33, +3.3, paired +26/-5/-11, inside the registered -25..+25 and above the pre-registered keep threshold 677.0, so keep. Pretraining went as predicted: 5,362/5,298/5,220 steps (186: 5,357/5,435/5,355; seed 2's pod reached step 1 at 38 s vs 33-35 s), the ~45 s curve point was already at val 0.133/0.119 on seeds 0/1 (200/149 steps; 186 s0 0.208 at 197), final val 0.092/0.082/0.086, held-out greedy 0.971/0.972/0.959 (186: 0.972/0.978/0.974). Surprise, the same pattern as 190: the RL start got much worse while the score did not. Round-1 targets solved 682/454/225 vs 186's 739/779/711 (sample acc 0.215/0.105/0.065 vs 0.272/0.290/0.231; total parse failures 45k/53k/54k vs 38k/41k/36k), so my registered falsifier (round 1 below 650 on 2+ seeds) fired. But RL closed the gap by round 2 on every seed (1023/926/943 vs 1003/1035/1004) and ended at 1189/1160/1142 (mean 1164 vs 1172), and the eval did not follow round 1 (seed 1 with 454 tied seed 0 with 682 at 694). Two runs now (190 init, 191 warmup) show early-trajectory changes that leave pretraining loss unchanged moving the round-1 harvest by hundreds while the final metric stays within seed noise: round 1 is a noisy, recoverable signal, not a proxy for the metric. Kept by the simplicity rule; +3.3 is ~0.2 SE and not evidence that no warmup is better. Seed 0's pod had a slow Lean gate (796 s vs 411-491) and finished at 2,019 s of 2,400.",
